WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.160
Gustav “s4” Magnusson was born on  April 1, 1992 in Sweden.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.420
As the youngest, s4 was often left to watch his three older brothers play video games.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.200
But while he was watching, s4 developed a
different kind of appreciation for the games

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.780
his brothers played.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.080
He was watching from the outside, analysing how they played so that when he finally got his turn,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.480
he was ahead of the curve.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.780
But as he grew older, S4's family came to realize that his dedication to games

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.560
wasn't different than other kids dedication to sports.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.660
But it was in Heroes of Newerth where s4 first made a name for himself.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.180
(Casting)

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.640
Alongside a slew of other future Dota 2 stars.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:38.660
(Casting)

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:43.980
He signed with Lions eSports Klubb and made
it all the way to the NASL Season 2 Finals,

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.900
but couldn’t attend due to visa issues.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.000
Just a few months later, s4 switched to Dota 2, and joined Team Empire during a period

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.440
of roster instability.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.980
Only months after joining, s4 left Empire
to form a new team, The Tough Bananas,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.140
which was quickly signed 
by the Copenhagen Wolves.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:12.080
(Casting)

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.300
But that roster didn’t find much success
either, leaving s4 to find yet another new team.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:25.660
This time, he formed No Tidehunter alongside Admiral Bulldog, Eternal Envy, Black and Kizzles.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:31.620
After some success, Black and Kizzle left
the team, and were replaced by Loda and Akke.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:37.200
s4 didn’t know it at the time, but he was
finally on a team that was set to make history.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:47.340
(Casting)

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.920
At DreamHack Winter 2012, No Tidehunter didn’t drop a match in the group stage, and beat

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:59.920
Evil Geniuses 2-1 in the grand finals thanks in part to an unbelievable strategy concocted by s4 himself.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:39.000
(Casting)

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.960
The now-iconic Roshan Bait single-handedly made s4 the captain of No Tidehunter,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:51.000
"How did you pitch that to your teammates, like 'hey guys we're gonna do this crazy strat that requires one of us

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.580
to die.' at the very beginning."

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:58.700
"So what happened is, we played the Grand Finals not on the main stage in game one.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.420
and then game two we had to go out to the main stage.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.840
But we had like a 30 minute break.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:09.420
So it was like, what are we gonna do guys? We're down one game, we're playing against EG.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.860
So yeah we just came up with a strat to get some momentum you know into the game."

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.860
"So, you actually came up with that strat on the spot."

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:17.520
"Yeah."

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.620
"You didn't see it was a pub strat or anything like that?"

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:28.720
"No we were actually, to start off trying to do a level one rush. But then, we came up with better ideas

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:29.800
as we went on."

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.580
But it wasn't the only thing about that tournament that propelled him into stardom.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.560
S4 was pioneering strategy and No Tidehunter were just outplaying their opponents.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:58.600
(Casting)

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:02.540
No Tidehunter took a Christmas break, but s4 kept playing.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.980
He participated in Beyond the Summit’s Fistful of Tangoes 1v1 tournament.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:12.700
"Good evening ladies and gentlemen, boys and girls, it is time for something completely different."

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.980
"That's right, we are going to try our hands at our first ever 1v1 tournament."

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:22.300
"It's called a Fistful of Tangoes, if you don't get the reference I suggest you do some googling."

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:28.360
He placed second, after a long loser’s bracket run where he beat KuroKy, Korok, SingSing and Synderen,

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:32.340
cementing his place as one of Dota 2’s best young talents.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:51.260
(Casting)

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:55.460
Once the tournament circuit restarted, No Tidehunter continued to do well,

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.440
winning StarLadder StarSeries Season 5 in April.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:16.540
(Casting)

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:22.220
Just after that, No Tidehunter was signed to Alliance, and the team went on an absolute tear.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:08:08.760
(Casting)

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.120
The team looked unstoppable.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:16.700
(Casting)

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:17.920
And they were.

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:37.940
(Casting)

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:42.900
"Good to have this much momentum, or are you guys like man maybe we're peaking very early,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:44.420
when it comes to this kind of thing."

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.660
"I think it's good.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.820
If you imagine being like a losing team, I don't think that would be feeling so good

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:53.920
going into another tournament."

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:54.920
"It's a good problem to have I guess."

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:55.600
"Yeah,

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:59.140
Alliance went 14-0 in TI3’s group stage.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:03.300
"Alliance, the team from Sweden. Undefeated in the prelims."

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.740
then moved on to drop just one game in the upper bracket.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:14.960
"Like, I always imagined how, it would be for the players in the games, what's the pressure and stuff like that."

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:19.780
"But now, getting to TI3, it's amazing."

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.260
But Natus Vincere put together an impressive run of their own

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.720
and were slated to face off against Alliance in the grand finals.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:32.400
"Ladies and gentlemen welcome to the Grand Finals of The International 3.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.840
(Cheering)

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:38.280
In game one, Na'Vi's Puppey brought out several unique picks.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:43.020
But S4 stuck to the usual gameplan. And they ended up stomping Na'Vi.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:52.200
(Casting)

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.180
The challenge was met with force, as Na'Vi took a quick game of their own.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:05.060
(Casting)

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:09.980
Despite the close series, Na'Vi put Dendi on Io in the mid lane for game three.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:15.340
(Casting)

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:18.840
Thanks to the pick. Na'Vi had control of the early game.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.860
And Alliance were firmly shut down.

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:34.880
(Casting)

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:39.740
With that Na'Vi were just one game away from winning TI3.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.880
And the chance for the majority of the roster, to carve their names on the  Aegis of Champions twice.

00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:51.460
Both teams opened game four with stable, well-rounded picks.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:54.940
But S4's ace came in the final pick with Night Stalker.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:11:13.360
(Casting)

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:19.400
With Alliance tying up the series 2-2, TI3 already broke new ground for a TI Grand Final.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:24.400
"This is the first time in TI history that we're going to a game number five."

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:29.600
Creative drafts and ballsy plays characterized the series, but the best was yet to come.

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:37.180
"It all comes down to this, one more game, $2 million dollars on the table."

00:11:37.600 --> 00:12:17.560
(Casting)

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:23.080
Alliance took the first Aegis at 21 minutes and stalled the game. Waiting for Roshan to reset.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:42.160
(Casting)

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:50.720
As Game 5 neared it’s end S4 showed up when it counted most and delivered a clutch Dream Coil, giving Alliance the chance to close it out.

00:12:50.800 --> 00:13:50.960
(Casting)

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.080
S4 stood at the very peak of Dota.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:21.000
He was a key player in the most important game ever played.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.040
(Casting)

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:27.640
He was the man behind the million dollar dream coils.

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:38.240
(Casting)

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:41.400
After TI3, teams started to catch up to Alliance.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:46.200
Na`Vi got their revenge on Alliance in a 3-1 grand finals at StarLadder Season 7.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:55.080
(Casting)

00:14:55.080 --> 00:15:02.600
Alliance then fell out of MLG Columbus in the group stage, and the team only managed to pick up two LAN wins between TI3 and TI4.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:07.640
They weren’t a terrible team, but they were a shell of their former selves.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:17.680
"Welcome to The International."

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:28.640
S4 won the 1v1 tournament at TI4, but it was the only victory he’d taste all tournament.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:48.000
(Casting)

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:52.080
Alliance went 6-9 in the group stage, tied for 11th.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:20.000
(Casting)

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:38.320
In the span of one year, Alliance went from unstoppable world champions to also-rans.

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.440
S4 decided to try his luck elsewhere.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:51.880
He teamed up with his longtime rival, legendary Na`Vi captain Puppey to help found Team Secret, alongside Fly, BigDaddyN0tail and Kuroky.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:57.960
On the surface, Secret looked like a dream team. Every player on the team was one of the best in their position.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:07.000
However, in there debut at Starladder Season 10 they would get stomped by none other than s4’s former team, Alliance.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:32.440
(Casting)

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:46.920
Eventually finding their footing, Team Secret would go on to close out the calendar year by placing 3rd/4th at ESL One New York, 2nd at StarLadder Season 10, 3rd at the Summit 2, 1st at XMG Captain’s Draft and 1st at Dota Pit League Season 2.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:55.440
(Casting)

00:17:56.400 --> 00:18:04.120
Over the holiday season, the team lost Fly and N0tail but replaced them with Arteezy and Zai, maintaining their status as a super team.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:17.880
(Casting)

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:24.520
Simply put, Secret were incredible, winning trophy after trophy in the lead-up to TI5.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:19:12.440
(Casting)

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:16.200
But it all fell apart at Ti.

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:31.360
(Casting)

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:41.640
Secret placed second in their group with a 4-3-0 record, but fell in the bracket, losing 2-0 to EHOME and 2-1 to Virtus.pro.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:50.080
For all his hard work, S4 had another mediocre TI performance on his record. It wasn’t terrible, but it wasn’t a win.

00:19:50.080 --> 00:20:03.120
(Casting)

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:09.360
Team Secret collapsed after the loss. Puppey was the only player who stuck with the org, and rebuilt its roster by himself.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:17.160
For his part, s4 went back to Alliance and over the course of the year, they rebuilt their TI3-winning squad.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:26.600
The team failed to put together any significant wins after TI, but in December, they picked up a massive win at World Cyber Arena 2015.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:58.480
(Casting)

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:08.520
And just a few weeks later, at StarLadder i-League Season 1, the old Alliance swept TI5 champions Evil Geniuses in the finals to take first place.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:22.480
(Casting)

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:28.080
Things starting looking up for the old heroes. But the nostalgic wave didn’t last long.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:38.240
Alliance didn’t find any more wins that year. They attended every Valve Major, and qualified for TI6, but were swept out of the bracket stage by EHOME and Fnatic.

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:49.600
(Casting)

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:54.520
While S4 was struggling, some of his old Secret teammates had formed OG.

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:59.200
s4 joined, reuniting with Fly and N0tail, and switched to the offlane.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:06.320
(Casting)

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:24.160
OG looked good as the season started, but they came into their own at the Boston Major, where s4 won his first ever Valve Major.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:48.720
(Casting)

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:55.000
For s4, a player who seemed past his prime, OG represented a shot at redemption.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:04.800
(Casting)

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:09.440
And OG kept up their momentum by taking first place at the Kiev Major.

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:25.080
(Casting)

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:31.440
S4 found redemption on OG. He found a team that was what Secret was supposed to be.

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:39.120
(Casting)

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:46.280
OG placed 7th-8th at TI that year, and while they’ve looked strong since, the team isn’t favored to win TI8.

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:50.920
But in Dota 2, TI is just about the only tournament that matters.

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:55.800
There are important events outside of TI, but none can match its prestige.

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:59.240
None have its multi-million dollar prize pool.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:07.840
None can award you the Aegis, and title of best in the world. And S4 wanted to be known as the best.

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:14.560
He knows what it means, what it feels like to stand at the top. And he won't rest until he feels it again.

